    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The <strong>1940s</strong> and <strong>1980s</strong> tie for the most diverse winner pools,
      each producing <strong>12 unique composers</strong> across 10 ceremonies. In both decades,
      only one ceremony went to a repeat winner — Max Steiner in 1943, and John Barry in 1985,
      respectively — suggesting these were periods of creative ferment where the Academy rewarded
      a broad spectrum of compositional voices rather than returning to established favorites.
    </p>
    <p>
      At the opposite extreme, the <strong>1950s</strong> are the most concentrated decade: only
      <strong>7 unique composers</strong> across 10 ceremonies. Three composers — Dimitri Tiomkin,
      Franz Waxman, and Alfred Newman — account for 6 of the decade's 10 wins, with Tiomkin claiming
      three alone. This concentration reflects the dominance of the established Hollywood studio system
      composers in the postwar era.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <strong>2010s</strong> stand as the only full decade (10 ceremonies) in which every single winner
      was a first-time recipient. This represents a historic break from earlier patterns and suggests
      the Academy's increasing willingness to reward new voices over returning champions.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- SECTION 4: ERA-BASED ANALYSIS -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="era-analysis">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-num">Section 4</span>
    <h2>Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lead">
      Grouping the 93 ceremonies into four distinct eras — Golden Age, Silver Age, Modern Era,
      and Contemporary Era — reveals how the profile of winning composers and the character of
      honored scores have shifted with the evolution of cinema itself.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Era cards -->
  <div class="era-card">
    <h3>Golden Age</h3>
    <div class="era-range">1934 – 1959 · 26 Ceremonies</div>
    <div class="era-stats">
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>25</strong> unique composers</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>Max Steiner</strong> top (3 wins)</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>96%</strong> first-time winners</div>
    </div>
    <p style="color:var(--text-dim);font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;">
      The Golden Age of Hollywood established the symphonic template for film scoring. The era's
      dominant figures — Max Steiner (3 wins), Dimitri Tiomkin (3), Alfred Newman (2), Miklos Rozsa (2),
      and Franz Waxman (2) — were European-trained composers who brought late-Romantic orchestral
      traditions to the screen. Winning films like <em>Gone with the Wind</em>-era epics,
      <em>Ben-Hur</em>, and <em>Spellbound</em> demanded large-scale, leitmotif-driven scores.
      With 25 unique winners across 26 ceremonies, this era had the lowest repeat rate of any
      era — only one ceremony (1943, Max Steiner) went to a previous winner. The craft was still
      being defined, and the Academy's recognition was spread broadly across the studio system's
      deep bench of talent.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="era-card">
    <h3>Silver Age</h3>
    <div class="era-range">1960 – 1979 · 20 Ceremonies</div>
    <div class="era-stats">
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>18</strong> unique composers</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>Jarre, Barry, Rota, Williams</strong> (2 each)</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>20%</strong> repeat-winner rate</div>
    </div>
    <p style="color:var(--text-dim);font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;">
      The Silver Age marked a transitional period as the studio system gave way to the New Hollywood.
      Maurice Jarre emerged as the era's defining voice with wins for <em>Lawrence of Arabia</em> (1962)
      and <em>Doctor Zhivago</em> (1965), while John Barry brought a cooler, jazz-inflected sensibility
      to wins for <em>Born Free</em> (1966) and <em>The Lion in Winter</em> (1968). John Williams's
      first two wins — <em>Jaws</em> (1975) and <em>Star Wars</em> (1977) — closed out the era by
      redefining the blockbuster score. The era also saw genre breakthroughs: Nino Rota's win for
      <em>The Godfather</em> (1972) brought operatic Italian scoring traditions to a gangster epic,
      while Giorgio Moroder's <em>Midnight Express</em> (1978) introduced electronic music to the
      Academy's recognition.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="era-card">
    <h3>Modern Era</h3>
    <div class="era-range">1980 – 1999 · 20 Ceremonies</div>
    <div class="era-stats">
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>18</strong> unique composers</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>Alan Menken</strong> top (3 wins)</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>15%</strong> repeat-winner rate</div>
    </div>
    <p style="color:var(--text-dim);font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;">
      The Modern Era was shaped by two poles: the continued dominance of symphonic giants and the
      rise of the animated musical. Alan Menken's three wins for Disney — <em>The Little Mermaid</em>
      (1989), <em>Beauty and the Beast</em> (1991), and <em>Aladdin</em> (1992) — marked the peak
      of the Disney Renaissance and made him the most-awarded composer of the era. John Williams
      added his fourth Oscar (<em>Schindler's List</em>, 1993) and John Barry his fourth
      (<em>Dances with Wolves</em>, 1990). The era also produced notable one-time winners who
      reflected diversifying tastes: Herbie Hancock for <em>Round Midnight</em> (1986), the first
      jazz musician to win in the category, and Hans Zimmer's first win for <em>The Lion King</em>
      (1994), signaling the arrival of a new blockbuster scoring aesthetic.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="era-card">
    <h3>Contemporary Era</h3>
    <div class="era-range">2000 – 2026 · 27 Ceremonies</div>
    <div class="era-stats">
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>22</strong> unique composers</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>Ludwig Göransson</strong> top (4 wins)</div>
      <div class="era-stat"><strong>22%</strong> repeat-winner rate</div>
    </div>
    <p style="color:var(--text-dim);font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;">
      The Contemporary Era is defined by unprecedented diversity — in composer backgrounds, musical
      styles, and the geographic origins of winning films. Ludwig Göransson has become the era's
      dominant figure with four wins in eight years (2018–2026), though his wins span remarkably
      different sound worlds: the Afrofuturist hip-hop orchestration of <em>Black Panther</em>,
      the atomically tense minimalism of <em>Oppenheimer</em>, and whatever <em>Sinners</em>
      brings in 2026. Howard Shore's two wins for <em>The Lord of the Rings</em> trilogy
      (2001, 2003) represent the last great symphonic fantasy score to dominate the category.
      The era has seen historic firsts: Hildur Guðnadóttir (2019, <em>Joker</em>) is the first
      identifiably female composer to win in the category's history based on the dataset. Trent Reznor
      and Atticus Ross (2010, 2020) brought industrial and electronic music backgrounds to the
      Oscar podium. A.R. Rahman (2008, <em>Slumdog Millionaire</em>) and Tan Dun (2000,
      <em>Crouching Tiger, Hidden Dragon</em>) brought non-Western musical traditions to the
      Academy's highest recognition.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-box">
