    <h2>Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lead">
      The nine-decade history of the Best Original Score award is punctuated by landmark moments —
      firsts, streaks, gaps, and records that illuminate the evolving landscape of film music.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-box">
    <h3>Milestones</h3>
    <ul class="milestone-list">
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1934</span>
        <div><strong>First award:</strong> Louis Silvers wins for <em>One Night of Love</em> at the 6th Academy Awards, inaugurating what would become one of Oscar's most prestigious categories.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1939</span>
        <div><strong>Most composers on one award:</strong> Four composers — Richard Hageman, W. Franke Harling, John Leipold, and Leo Shuken — share the Oscar for <em>Stagecoach</em>, the largest collaborative win in the category's history.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1951</span>
        <div><strong>First back-to-back winner:</strong> Franz Waxman wins consecutive awards for <em>Sunset Boulevard</em> (1950) and <em>A Place in the Sun</em> (1951), the first composer to achieve this feat.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1994</span>
        <div><strong>Longest gap between wins (27 years):</strong> Hans Zimmer wins for <em>The Lion King</em> in 1994 and would not win again until <em>Dune</em> in 2021 — the longest interval between Oscars for any composer in the category's history.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2006</span>
        <div><strong>Second back-to-back winner:</strong> Gustavo Santaolalla wins consecutive awards for <em>Brokeback Mountain</em> (2005) and <em>Babel</em> (2006), the second composer after Waxman to achieve consecutive victories.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2019</span>
        <div><strong>First female winner:</strong> Hildur Guðnadóttir wins for <em>Joker</em>, becoming the first identifiably female composer to win Best Original Score in the category's 85-year history at that point.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2024</span>
        <div><strong>Same film wins twice:</strong> Ludwig Göransson's score for <em>Oppenheimer</em> wins at both the 2023 and 2024 ceremonies — the only instance in the dataset of the same film winning in consecutive years.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2026</span>
        <div><strong>Most recent winner:</strong> Ludwig Göransson wins his fourth Oscar for <em>Sinners</em>, cementing his position as one of the three most-awarded composers in Oscar history alongside John Barry and John Williams — achieved in just eight years.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

