<!-- SECTION 6: CONCLUSIONS -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="conclusions">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-num">Section 6</span>
    <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="takeaway-grid">
    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <div class="num">01</div>
      <h4>The Concentration Paradox</h4>
      <p>
        While 79 unique composers have won, the top 17 repeat winners account for a disproportionate
        share of the podium. Three composers now sit at four wins each, and the repeat winner rate
        of 28% means repeat dominance is a structural feature of the category, not an anomaly.
        Yet the 2010s — where every single winner was a first-timer — proves that eras of
        unprecedented openness are possible.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <div class="num">02</div>
      <h4>Eras of Diversity Alternate with Eras of Consolidation</h4>
      <p>
        The 1940s and 1980s produced 12 unique winners each (maximum diversity), while the 1950s
        produced only 7 (maximum concentration). This oscillation suggests that periods of
        stylistic consolidation — where a few composers define the sound of an era — are regularly
        disrupted by creative resurgences that broaden the winner pool.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <div class="num">03</div>
      <h4>Göransson's Unprecedented Pace</h4>
      <p>
        Ludwig Göransson's four wins in eight years (2018–2026) is the fastest accumulation in
        Oscar scoring history. By comparison, John Barry's four wins spanned 24 years and John
        Williams's four spanned 18. Göransson's trajectory, fueled by collaborations with A-list
        directors working across genres, may signal a new model of composer career acceleration
        in the franchise era.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <div class="num">04</div>
      <h4>The Expanding Definition of "Score"</h4>
      <p>
        The winning sound has moved decisively beyond the symphonic orchestra. From Giorgio Moroder's
        synthesizers (1978) to Trent Reznor and Atticus Ross's industrial soundscapes (2010, 2020),
        from A.R. Rahman's Bollywood-inflected <em>Slumdog Millionaire</em> (2008) to Jon Batiste's
        jazz-meets-hip-hop contributions to <em>Soul</em> (2020), the Academy has steadily broadened
        what qualifies as an "original score." The symphonic tradition that defined the Golden Age
        remains a minority of contemporary winners.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <div class="num">05</div>
      <h4>Looking Ahead: The Late 2020s and Beyond</h4>
      <p>
        With three composers tied at four wins, the all-time record is within reach for any of them.
        Ludwig Göransson, still early in his career trajectory, is positioned to potentially break
        the record. The increasing globalization of film production — reflected in recent winners
        from Iceland, Germany, India, and China — suggests the category will continue to diversify
        geographically. The question heading into the late 2020s is whether the category will see
        another consolidation around a small group of repeat winners, as in the 1950s, or continue
        the 2010s pattern of rewarding fresh voices.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

